after more than eight years of  on-and-off cease-fires and being forced  from their homes many times these  refugees seemed unfazed by turkeys  announcement of a five-day pause in  fighting the war in Syria has shown them  time after time peace is never assured  mother Liz Mejia  this life is not called life anymore  it's called destruction if anyone says  I'm alive what he should really say is I  am dead in the last five days  the UN's international organization of  migration has screened more than 2,300  Syrian refugees smuggled into Iraq  refugees say they have no other option  because Kurdish fighters in Syria are  preventing them from crossing the border  we are Syrian but we hate our country we  don't want to go back nedra and her  sister each paid a smuggler six hundred  dollars she says she and her two young  children walked for five days I was  alone in the desert everybody left it  was just me and my children I was  suffering this latest wave joins the  more than 1 million refugees and  internally displaced people already here  in the Kurdish region of northern Iraq  the government says the international  funding it relies on has been cut in  half it's affecting its ability to  provide basic services such as food  water education and health care  the influx is only adding to the  financial pressure  the French Foreign Minister met the  president of the Kurdistan Regional  Government on Thursday and pledged about  11 million dollars to alleviate the  humanitarian emergency it's very sad  actually this is too much  we hope the warring parties will get  together and talk about peace again and  exercise maximum restraint again none of  the refugees we spoke to are thinking  about returning they've lost all hope  that Syria will ever be the home they  remembered before the war  Natacha guy named al jazeera - Hook  province in the Kurdish region of  northern Iraq
 

